Hi Iraida

Following on from the answer of Fred. You may be interested in the new package nimbleNoBounds <https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/6Wb7T29pun4>  which provides a set of transformed distributions with exactly this in mind.  In your case I would expect that using this package could help increase the effective sample size. However, I'm not optimistic that it would remove the bi-modality in your case.

Concerning multi-modal posteriors, the package nimbleAPT <https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/K8RN_73p5-0/m/8JjAxYrcBAAJ> has been designed to provide more efficient sampling (i.e. increased frequency of between-mode jumps, thus higher effective sample size). That said, it is unclear whether or not this would provide a significant improvement in efficiency in your case, because the multiple modes are not separated by low probability zones - on the flip side, their variances are very different - so perhaps the extra effort can be worth it.

Concerning the interpretation of the multi-modality... that's hard to answer, although it often arises in ecological models. Perhaps you could try to dissect which elements of the data set are fitted well under low and high values of psi_maleT[3]. One approach would be to look at the LogProbs of various data nodes under low and high values of psi_maleT[3] and try to identify what (if anything) changes when switching from the low mode to the high mode. Alternatively (and probably simpler), for whatever plots you're generating from the model output, you could use two different colours for representing low and high modes of psi_maleT[3] respectively.

Best regards
David

